  "passage": {
    "title": "A Career in Nursing",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Nursing is one of the largest professions in the world, with more than twenty-seven million nurses. Their work is varied: some care for patients in hospitals, others visit people at home, and some work in schools, prisons or on ships. What they have in common is that they help people when those people need help most."
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "To become a nurse in most countries, you need to study at university for three or four years. The course includes classroom lessons, but at least half of the time is spent in hospitals and clinics, working with real patients under the supervision of experienced nurses. Students learn how to take blood pressure, give injections, clean wounds and, equally importantly, how to talk to frightened people."
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "A typical shift in a hospital lasts twelve hours, and many nurses work at night or at weekends. The work is physically demanding, because nurses spend most of the day on their feet and often have to lift patients. It can also be emotionally difficult. Nurses see people in pain, and sometimes they lose patients. Many say that support from colleagues is the most important thing that helps them cope."
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Despite these difficulties, most nurses love their job. A recent survey found that eight out of ten would choose the same career again. They say the best part is seeing patients get better and receiving thanks from families. Nurses can also choose a speciality, such as children's nursing or emergency care, and there are good opportunities to become managers or teachers."
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Unfortunately, many countries do not have enough nurses. The number of older people is growing, so the need will continue to rise. Governments are trying to attract more people by improving pay, offering scholarships and making conditions better. For anyone who likes practical work and helping others, nursing can be a rewarding career."
      }
    ]
  },
